## Standalone Minimum Gate

This repository must remain usable without personal global Agent rules or optional global Skills:

1. Before editing, summarize the goal, in/out of scope, acceptance criteria, affected modules/files, and ambiguities/risks. Ask before editing when ambiguity changes behavior, contracts, data, permissions, billing, security, or workflow.
2. Before a concept enters a public contract, persistent model/state, security/data boundary, or cross-Owner lifecycle, consult the project's terminology registry. Register only independently identified/lifecycled concepts, cross-Owner/public/persistent contracts, security/data boundaries, or demonstrated naming collisions. Record a stable Concept ID, canonical label, precise boundary/Owner and key invariants; aliases/confusables only when real. Local fields/helpers/types, UI-only states, derived attributes, single-Owner implementation details and standard technical terms do not require global registration. Existing concepts are referenced rather than copied, and an unresolved identity ambiguity blocks only its dependent path.
3. If the repository uses Domain SDD, query the matching Domain closure first: Domain Metadata, PRD, SPEC, Logic Flow, Test Flow, Dictionary terms, direct Contract/Data dependencies and Acceptance Boundaries. Do not maintain a second context-route schema or copy technical details into Domain PRD/SPEC.
4. Treat assumptions as temporary working hypotheses. Before implementation, business acceptance expectations, canonical documentation, or delivery, resolve every material assumption as verified evidence or an explicit user decision; otherwise remove the assumption and clean up dependent plans, tests, docs, and edits. Any remaining hypothesis must be non-material, harmless, reversible, unable to affect behavior or risk, and state its checkpoint. Preserve a non-blocking unknown only as an open question or evidence gap without a presumed answer.
5. Keep established security baselines, but bind any new non-trivial control to an accepted protected asset, threat or failure, attacker capability, trust boundary, and observable guarantee. Do not silently upgrade protection against mistakes into protection against compromised administrators, malicious insiders, or supply-chain substitution. Describe audit labels, approvals, identifiers, digests, and signatures only by the guarantee they actually provide. New identities, roles, approval states, integrity mechanisms, key lifecycles, migrations, or cross-Owner enforcement require an explicit material decision; otherwise report the limitation as residual risk without expanding the task.
6. Inspect the existing Owner, similar implementations, reusable modules, dependency boundaries, business acceptance locations, generators, and verification commands. Do not add parallel logic without justification.
7. For bugs, preserve a focused pre-fix failure and repeat it after the fix; preserve automated verification only when the project contract or release gate explicitly owns it and it protects a stable observable boundary. For features, verify only affected happy, invalid, edge, permission/state, and nearby regression cases. Start with focused verification, broaden once when cross-owner risk requires it, and do not run both an aggregate gate and its contained targets on unchanged inputs merely to repeat the same suites.
8. Before delivery, report requirement match, design/reuse decisions, changed and generated files, commands and results, remaining risks, and rollback/recovery notes when relevant.

Optional global rules may provide stricter or more detailed guidance when installed; this section is the repository-local fallback, not a second full engineering standard.
